In article <588@watserv1.waterloo.edu> mdhardin@watserv1.waterloo.edu (Matthew D. Harding) writes:
>Hi, I have some generic question on setting up Nethack 3.0, pl6, for OS/2.
>Specifically:
>
>I downloaded this executable from a BBS. Is there any way to find out from
>the executable what options were enabled at compile time? Does the OS/2 port
>still have restrictions on size of exceutable, like the PC version used to have?i.e. is it safe to assume that due to OS/2's dynamic memory management all
>options were compiled in?

Very difficult to find out from the compiled executable which game options
are enabled.  If one knows the game, the best way is to play and observe
what is missing.  OS/2 certainly can support all NH features now and in the
(near) future.

>Also, it came with a record file. Editing entries with a normal text editor
>results in some garbage when I play the game and save to the record file. I
>can't exactly describe it, but there must be some control characters or some
>formatting done in the file. Can anyone describe to me the format of the
>record file? I remember being able to do this in nethack 2.2's record file. 
>Also, why can't I delete the record file and get hack to write me a fresh one?
>I get an error saying it can't write to c:\hack\recordrecord {sic}, yet no
>where have I defined where my record file is. Can you point to this in the
>..cnf files at all?

This is certainly a bug that requires remedial actions.  If you want to make
a fresh record file, create a file with some short text string in it (e.g.,
"foobar"), and rename it "record".  Now NH can find and recognize it.

>And finally, can someone tell me where the source is kept, and whether there
>are exceutables stored somewhere or do I have to find an OS/2 compiler? (Ugh).

Sources for the latest patchlevel are available using anonymous ftp at least
from linc.cis.upenn.edu, directory pub/NH3.0.  Can't give you any pointer to
OS/2 binaries, sorry.  To compile NH under OS/2 MSC 5.1 can be used.

